WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.870 --> 00:00:02.640
- I got a question from Luna.

00:00:02.640 --> 00:00:05.250
"How should I configure
permissions for my Cloud Run

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:06.460
Service?"

00:00:06.460 --> 00:00:09.600
Well, Cloud Run comes
with two gatekeepers.

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:13.950
The first one keeps track of who
can trigger the service to run.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.239
If I'm writing a
public web app or API,

00:00:16.239 --> 00:00:20.130
I set this to allow
unauthenticated invocations.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.410
For internal,
non-public services,

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.870
I set this to require
authentication.

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:28.290
So only accounts with a Cloud
Run invocation permission

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:29.970
can trigger it.

00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:32.490
The second gatekeeper
keeps track of what

00:00:32.490 --> 00:00:34.290
the service is allowed to do.

00:00:34.290 --> 00:00:36.810
By default, Cloud Run uses
a service account, which

00:00:36.810 --> 00:00:38.880
has permissions to
access databases,

00:00:38.880 --> 00:00:40.950
file storage, et cetera.

00:00:40.950 --> 00:00:43.020
This makes it easy
to get started.

00:00:43.020 --> 00:00:44.760
But for a production
service, you

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:46.650
should create a
custom service account

00:00:46.650 --> 00:00:49.080
that only has the minimum
permissions for the service

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.560
to do its job.

00:00:50.560 --> 00:00:52.140
This reduces the
amount of damage

00:00:52.140 --> 00:00:55.950
that an attack or a
stupid mistake can cause.

00:00:55.950 --> 00:00:57.860
Happy coding.

